WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.825
- So looks like, Paul,
you've got

00:00:01.825 --> 00:00:03.747
3ds Max open here,

00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:05.849
a couple of beautiful
looking teapots.

00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:07.701
We're talking about rotations,

00:00:07.701 --> 00:00:10.112
and you mentioned you rotate
with X, Y, Z.

00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:12.118
- Yeah.
- So maybe you can show us

00:00:12.118 --> 00:00:13.905
how that looks in the program.

00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:15.425
- And the problems with it.

00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:18.092
So, yeah, so there's, you know,
a few ways of handling,

00:00:18.092 --> 00:00:21.018
you know, roll,
pitch, yaw, and whatnot,

00:00:21.018 --> 00:00:23.284
but X, Y, Z,
same kind of idea.

00:00:23.284 --> 00:00:26.545
The problem is,
is there are issues with it.

00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:28.378
I'm going to try
and explain that.

00:00:28.378 --> 00:00:30.813
So gimbal lock is

00:00:30.813 --> 00:00:34.012
a problem that happens
to animators all the time

00:00:34.012 --> 00:00:36.160
as they're animating characters
and doing things.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.891
And what'll happen is, all of
a sudden, something will

00:00:37.891 --> 00:00:39.440
wig out in between frames.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.808
As you scrub from one key
 to the next key,

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:44.775
things look like they're
going all over the place.

00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:46.370
Why is that happening, right?

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.255
So let's rotate this teapot
and not have this.

00:00:49.255 --> 00:00:51.566
So I'm rotating
 in a local space,

00:00:51.566 --> 00:00:53.895
which really
doesn't exist per se,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.460
And you'll see
the axes roll over.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.567
So I'll head along
to frame 30 here,

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.138
turn on animate,
and what I'm going to do is

00:01:04.138 --> 00:01:06.356
I'm going to start
with rotating around,

00:01:06.356 --> 00:01:08.740
you know,
the perceived X axis.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.580
I'm going to rotate 90 degrees.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.455
I'm just going to turn
my angle snap there.

00:01:12.455 --> 00:01:14.455
I'm going to rotate
over 90 degrees.

00:01:14.455 --> 00:01:16.509
I'm going to head along
 to frame 60,

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.024
and I'm going to grab, you know,

00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:20.831
what is now the Z
axis locally, and I'm going to

00:01:20.831 --> 00:01:23.335
rotate that, and we'll take
a look at thatanimation.

00:01:23.335 --> 00:01:25.570
So that animation
looks exactly like

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.090
what you'd expect it to do.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.090
I rotate it over 90 degrees.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.991
I rotate it another 90 degrees.

00:01:30.991 --> 00:01:32.610
All's good.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.010
Well, let's do it a little
slightly different here.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.507
So frame 30 again.

00:01:37.507 --> 00:01:39.819
I'm going to grab
the red teapot now,

00:01:39.819 --> 00:01:42.540
and this time, I'm going to
go around the Y axis,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.227
and I'm going to
do the same thing.

00:01:44.227 --> 00:01:46.640
So I'm going to
rotate it 90 degrees,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.725
and then I'm going to go
along to frame 60.

00:01:48.725 --> 00:01:51.340
I'm going to grab it in
what is now the Z axis,

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.900
what looks like
the Z axis again.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.980
I'm going to rotate
it over 90 degrees.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.893
And then as I scrub,
 what you'll notice is

00:01:56.893 --> 00:01:58.783
that red teapot is doing some

00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:02.240
really funky little thing,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.375
and it's like, why on Earth
is it doing that?

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:07.744
This is a big problem
that animators run into.

00:02:07.744 --> 00:02:10.615
We're rotating things
in a local axis,

00:02:10.615 --> 00:02:13.375
which doesn't actually exist.

00:02:13.375 --> 00:02:15.730
Kind of everything works
in parent space,

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.444
but Euler rotations
got their own

00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:20.440
hierarchy of things going on.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.771
And we can see
that by changing

00:02:22.771 --> 00:02:25.305
into a gimbal.

00:02:25.305 --> 00:02:27.130
And so if I look at
this first one,

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.776
and we're looking at
the X, Y, Z,

00:02:29.776 --> 00:02:32.312
you know,
the rotation here,

00:02:32.312 --> 00:02:35.106
and we skip through them,
you can see that,

00:02:35.106 --> 00:02:37.719
when it rotated around X,
you can see that

00:02:37.719 --> 00:02:40.535
Z and Y never
went anywhere.

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:43.895
They don't they don't appear
to be changing at all.

00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:46.352
But then when I got
to frame 30,

00:02:46.352 --> 00:02:48.116
and if you watch
what happens there,

00:02:48.116 --> 00:02:49.661
you can actually see the X,

00:02:49.661 --> 00:02:51.660
the red one now,
is starting to

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.761
flatten over onto Z,

00:02:54.761 --> 00:02:57.294
and it vanishes.

00:02:57.294 --> 00:02:59.787
Well, we just ran
into gimbal lock,

00:02:59.787 --> 00:03:02.605
but didn't go any further,
so we never saw the problem.

00:03:02.605 --> 00:03:05.449
This one, we started with

00:03:05.449 --> 00:03:08.403
the middle axis, the Y axis.
We rotated over.

00:03:08.403 --> 00:03:12.365
Watch what happens to
the red rotation there.

00:03:12.365 --> 00:03:14.608
It gets dropped flat onto

00:03:14.608 --> 00:03:17.180
what is Z.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.232
Now, you might say, well,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.360
what I actually did one,
then at frame 60,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.140
is I grabbed that Z axis.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.814
Well, if I look at
the local axis,

00:03:25.814 --> 00:03:28.066
you can see that
there's this imaginary

00:03:28.066 --> 00:03:30.165
Z axis I can grab.

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:32.677
But if we look at it
from Gimbal,

00:03:32.677 --> 00:03:35.383
which is really the math,

00:03:35.383 --> 00:03:37.751
what the math is doing,
in a sense, right?

00:03:37.751 --> 00:03:40.085
There is no axis
to grab there.

00:03:40.085 --> 00:03:42.354
And you can see that X is
pointing straight down,

00:03:42.354 --> 00:03:44.370
Y is pointing straight up.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.646
What on Earth
is going on, right?

00:03:46.646 --> 00:03:49.153
You know, we've taken our...
and we're kind of

00:03:49.153 --> 00:03:51.250
tearing it apart, you know, 
sort of thing.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.564
So as we start
to come out of that,

00:03:53.564 --> 00:03:55.698
what you'll see now is that to

00:03:55.698 --> 00:03:57.790
get to the end result,

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.781
because we're going to get
to the end result we wanted,

00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:02.833
you can see it kind of
trying to unwind itself,

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:06.021
unscrew those wacky axes

00:04:06.021 --> 00:04:08.027
so they actually end up

00:04:08.027 --> 00:04:10.248
back together again.

00:04:10.248 --> 00:04:13.364
So that gimbal problem
 in between here

00:04:13.364 --> 00:04:16.440
gets unwound along the way.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.755
Now, if you look at
the function curves,

00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:21.529
you know, those curves that
animators want to see,

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.285
the first teapot, the green one
makes complete sense.

00:04:24.285 --> 00:04:26.685
We rotate it over in X.

00:04:26.685 --> 00:04:29.925
You can see the X changing
until frame 30,

00:04:29.925 --> 00:04:32.902
and then we rotate it around Y,

00:04:32.902 --> 00:04:35.621
and it made total sense, okay?

00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:38.220
It ends up where we want.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.658
But if we look at the red teapot
in the same graph,

00:04:41.658 --> 00:04:43.634
well, there is

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.435
Y changing,

00:04:46.435 --> 00:04:49.235
and then I grabbed Z
and changed it.

00:04:49.235 --> 00:04:51.315
But look at Y going
back down again.

00:04:51.315 --> 00:04:53.400
So to try and unwind

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.828
the Euler math and get

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.188
to the end result,
it had to

00:04:58.188 --> 00:05:01.177
bring the Y axis
back down to zero again,

00:05:01.177 --> 00:05:04.360
causing this wacky,

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.534
you know,
problem in there, right?

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:08.798
So when we're setting
 things up,

00:05:08.798 --> 00:05:10.709
like a character, for instance,

00:05:10.709 --> 00:05:13.403
we're we're able to
sort of do things that get us

00:05:13.403 --> 00:05:17.205
around ever getting
into that problem.

00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:19.157
But if you think
about a character, let's say,

00:05:19.157 --> 00:05:21.516
doing a floor
gymnastics routine

00:05:21.516 --> 00:05:23.565
in the Olympics,

00:05:23.565 --> 00:05:26.205
and they're spinning
and flipping, their waist is

00:05:26.205 --> 00:05:29.446
constantly going through this
gimbaling problem, right?

00:05:29.446 --> 00:05:31.782
It's got to keep
running into it, you know?

00:05:31.782 --> 00:05:35.280
An Euler rotation
has an axis order.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.160
And so the axis order
by default is X, Y, Z.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.013
And what that means is
Z is the parent,

00:05:40.013 --> 00:05:42.143
Y is parented to Z,

00:05:42.143 --> 00:05:44.244
X is parented
to Y, effectively,

00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:45.751
if you want to
think of it that way.

00:05:45.751 --> 00:05:48.594
What's happening here
if I grab Z,

00:05:48.594 --> 00:05:50.362
and I'll just work
in local axis,

00:05:50.362 --> 00:05:52.779
and I've locked it all out
so I can only rotate this around

00:05:52.779 --> 00:05:54.880
that axis, you'll see that

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.310
when I rotate Z,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:59.862
Y and X are
rotating with them.

00:05:59.862 --> 00:06:01.554
They're children of it.

00:06:01.554 --> 00:06:04.301
If I grab just X,
so R, G, B, X, Y, Z,

00:06:04.301 --> 00:06:06.891
and rotate it, you'll notice

00:06:06.891 --> 00:06:10.316
nothing's actually happening
to the other two rings.

00:06:10.316 --> 00:06:14.285
The other two axes, in a sense,
aren't changing here.

00:06:14.285 --> 00:06:17.114
But if I grab Y and rotate it,

00:06:17.114 --> 00:06:19.846
it's got that problem
where all of a sudden we find

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:22.983
the X rotates down flat,

00:06:22.983 --> 00:06:25.005
and we've entered gimbal lock.

00:06:25.005 --> 00:06:27.591
So if I then got to that point
 and wanted to

00:06:27.591 --> 00:06:29.725
rotate around this axis,

00:06:29.725 --> 00:06:32.905
I would have to then go
 and do something

00:06:32.905 --> 00:06:35.669
to unwind it to get myself

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:38.302
to that point
that I can actually...

00:06:38.302 --> 00:06:40.146
You know, like, who knows

00:06:40.146 --> 00:06:41.706
what I'd have to do
to get there, right?

00:06:41.706 --> 00:06:43.970
Like, I'd have to go 
 and grab multiple

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.235
to get back to that
same solution again.

00:06:46.235 --> 00:06:47.735
So that's gimbal.

00:06:47.735 --> 00:06:49.275
- So if I understand correctly,

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.125
with those Euler angles,
you can

00:06:51.125 --> 00:06:53.175
represent an orientation.

00:06:53.175 --> 00:06:54.847
Like, the start and
the end were fine.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.354
- And the start and end's fine.

00:06:56.354 --> 00:06:57.856
- But it's getting in between.

00:06:57.856 --> 00:06:59.598
- And getting from one
 to the other,

00:06:59.598 --> 00:07:01.315
unless you start
keying every frame.

00:07:01.315 --> 00:07:03.210
So for animators,
 there's also things like

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.039
Euler filters, they call it.

00:07:05.039 --> 00:07:06.671
and it's,
you know, a little utility

00:07:06.671 --> 00:07:09.460
that tries to fix the problem.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.065
It doesn't always work,

00:07:11.065 --> 00:07:13.071
because it's
not a fixable problem.

00:07:13.071 --> 00:07:14.604
- It's part of the math.

00:07:14.604 --> 00:07:16.185
- Yeah, and it's something
we can just kind of hide.

00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:18.487
- Yes.
- You know...

00:07:18.487 --> 00:07:20.076
- You said you had to hide it.

00:07:20.076 --> 00:07:21.763
The problem he's
bumping into here is

00:07:21.763 --> 00:07:23.465
this X, Y, Z
you're talking about.

00:07:23.465 --> 00:07:25.150
- Yeah, entirely.
- From my perspective,

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:28.013
you have to multiply
those three matrices

00:07:28.013 --> 00:07:31.648
and the order of
matrix multiplication matters.

00:07:31.648 --> 00:07:34.272
And so what you've
effectively done is you've taken

00:07:34.272 --> 00:07:37.480
two of the axes 
and made them collinear.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.216
And so now you've got...

00:07:39.216 --> 00:07:41.332
You used to have three axes,
now you have two.

00:07:41.332 --> 00:07:42.855
- I guess.

00:07:42.855 --> 00:07:44.770
- Yeah, because you've taken
two of them combined,

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.762
and they're both pointing
in the same direction.

00:07:46.762 --> 00:07:48.360
- You know, for an animator,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.198
it's the idea that,

00:07:50.198 --> 00:07:52.208
you know, other methods
 like a quaternion

00:07:52.208 --> 00:07:54.508
doesn't give you this ability

00:07:54.508 --> 00:07:56.796
to be able to control
a function curve.

00:07:56.796 --> 00:07:59.010
So with a function curve,
for instance, you know,

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.888
I could grab them, and I could
 all make them all

00:08:00.888 --> 00:08:02.460
fast out, fast in.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.875
And it changes the timing
 between keys

00:08:04.875 --> 00:08:06.867
to be able to get things to...

00:08:06.867 --> 00:08:09.863
a character to emote
properly, whatever, right?

00:08:09.863 --> 00:08:12.795
Animators want it
to be able to control timing.

00:08:12.795 --> 00:08:14.985
The problem is, inevitably,
they always come to me and go,

00:08:14.985 --> 00:08:16.903
Paul, what on Earth
is going on?

00:08:16.903 --> 00:08:20.123
The software
is broken and whatever.

00:08:20.123 --> 00:08:21.633
And it's like, no.
- It's not.

00:08:21.633 --> 00:08:23.730
- It's just math, you know.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.668
- And I know this problem,
 gimbal lock,

00:08:25.668 --> 00:08:27.309
it can be a matter of
life and death.

00:08:27.309 --> 00:08:29.908
- Oh, Apollo 13, that's what
you're talking about.

00:08:29.908 --> 00:08:32.824
Yeah, when they were trying
 to come back in after

00:08:32.824 --> 00:08:35.128
the oxygen tank blew up,
they had to circle out

00:08:35.128 --> 00:08:37.205
around the moon, come back in,
 and they had to

00:08:37.205 --> 00:08:39.565
shut all the system down
because they had no air left.

00:08:39.565 --> 00:08:41.420
But they were a little bit
off trajectory, and they had to

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.085
make a little
adjustment to that.

00:08:43.085 --> 00:08:45.005
And the problem was they had
to steer that on their own,

00:08:45.005 --> 00:08:46.925
just eyeball it.

00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:48.738
The way that they can tell
the orientation

00:08:48.738 --> 00:08:50.457
of the spacecraft
in those days was to put it

00:08:50.457 --> 00:08:52.365
in exactly this kind
of a system.

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:55.040
But instead of the teapot,
that would be the gyroscope.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.800
It would be sitting
in the middle of all that.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.379
And the thing got into gimbal
 lock, and they just

00:08:58.379 --> 00:08:59.954
had to kind of, oh,
well, eyeball it.

00:08:59.954 --> 00:09:01.840
But they made it back.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.047
- Wow, yeah, I've seen
images of a big

00:09:04.047 --> 00:09:07.677
flashing button that says,
"gimbal lock."

00:09:07.677 --> 00:09:10.219
- You being so young, you could
only see it in the movies.

00:09:10.219 --> 00:09:12.671
- Yes, which movie was it in?
- Apollo 13.

00:09:12.671 --> 00:09:14.395
(laughing)

00:09:14.395 --> 00:09:16.729
- I actually watched that one.
- Yeah, I saw it on the telly.

00:09:16.729 --> 00:09:18.335
- Now, this wasn't a problem

00:09:18.335 --> 00:09:19.934
for the Artemis II mission,

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:21.690
the Orion spacecraft.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.197
- Yeah, no.
 We dropped the darn gimbal

00:09:23.197 --> 00:09:25.370
stuff, and yaw,
pitch, and roll.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:26.921
Let's get into quaternions,
do it right.

00:09:26.921 --> 00:09:28.447
- Yeah, so...

00:09:28.447 --> 00:09:30.890
- There were no animators
steering the boat.

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.015
(laughing)

00:09:33.015 --> 00:09:34.811
- So, yeah, thankfully,
 there's no flashing

00:09:34.811 --> 00:09:37.296
gimbal lock issues now
with spacecraft.

00:09:37.296 --> 00:09:40.040
- But quaternions, instead
of three numbers,

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.673
Paul's been talking about...

00:09:41.673 --> 00:09:43.483
Well, we say yaw, pitch, roll,

00:09:43.483 --> 00:09:45.599
but you've been saying
X, Y, Z.

00:09:45.599 --> 00:09:47.238
- Yep.
- But with a quaternion,

00:09:47.238 --> 00:09:49.320
it has four numbers.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.017
And the technical jargon is
 that having that

00:09:51.017 --> 00:09:54.052
extra number allows you
to not have a...

00:09:54.052 --> 00:09:55.893
It was like
a degree of freedom

00:09:55.893 --> 00:09:58.376
that you lost when
the two rings

00:09:58.376 --> 00:10:00.210
collapsed onto each other.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:01.918
And also with quaternions,

00:10:01.918 --> 00:10:05.145
it's not a chain of rotations.

00:10:05.145 --> 00:10:07.785
You're rotating
by X, Y, Z

00:10:07.785 --> 00:10:10.265
in a certain order,
but a quaternion just

00:10:10.265 --> 00:10:12.905
has one axis and one angle.

00:10:12.905 --> 00:10:14.585
- One arbitrary axis.

00:10:14.585 --> 00:10:16.585
- Yeah, it could be any axis.

00:10:16.585 --> 00:10:19.083
And, actually, yeah,
there's another representation

00:10:19.083 --> 00:10:20.885
of rotations called just

00:10:20.885 --> 00:10:22.450
axis angle.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.090
Has four numbers.
It doesn't gimbal lock,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.251
axis angle, but it's
not as nice

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:28.770
as quaternions.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.810
The nice thing about
quaternions, you could...

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:32.850
you can chain
rotation together.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.552
You just multiply
the quaternions.

00:10:34.552 --> 00:10:37.141
But as you were mentioning,
 quaternions aren't so

00:10:37.141 --> 00:10:39.186
friendly for animators.

00:10:39.186 --> 00:10:40.702
- No, you end up...

00:10:40.702 --> 00:10:42.890
You can't visualize

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.635
the axis angle idea
because of...

00:10:45.635 --> 00:10:47.766
You've got a ball
that's rolling straight,

00:10:47.766 --> 00:10:50.490
in a straight line, you know,
along the Y axis.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.515
It's rotating around X

00:10:52.515 --> 00:10:55.210
360 degrees and onwards.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:57.383
That makes sense in,

00:10:57.383 --> 00:10:58.970
you know, an Euler.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.099
You'll see one axis climbing

00:11:01.099 --> 00:11:03.086
its value as it rolls forward.

00:11:03.086 --> 00:11:04.602
Quaternion looks like this.

00:11:04.602 --> 00:11:06.122
We want to look
at the function curves.

00:11:06.122 --> 00:11:07.736
It's just a mess of numbers

00:11:07.736 --> 00:11:09.255
that make no sense to...

00:11:09.255 --> 00:11:12.268
- I hate to abandon
my friendship here with Umer,

00:11:12.268 --> 00:11:14.679
but he's the only guy I know

00:11:14.679 --> 00:11:16.949
that can look at
a quaternion and say,

00:11:16.949 --> 00:11:18.890
oh, I know what that's 
going to do.

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:20.991
And I have to confess
 when I look at your

00:11:20.991 --> 00:11:23.281
Euler angles, I go,
I know exactly

00:11:23.281 --> 00:11:25.290
where the teapot's
going to end up.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.099
I can just glance at
that and go, I get it.

00:11:27.099 --> 00:11:28.602
- Makes sense.
- I get that.

00:11:28.602 --> 00:11:30.170
He comes up with
all these numbers, I don't know,

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.565
it's divided by pi
or something.

00:11:32.565 --> 00:11:35.697
- And even though I love
quaternions, Scott,

00:11:35.697 --> 00:11:37.585
eventually, you need matrices.

00:11:37.585 --> 00:11:39.826
So you actually
turn the quaternion

00:11:39.826 --> 00:11:41.585
into a rotation matrix.

00:11:41.585 --> 00:11:43.250
- Just to a simple matrix, yeah.

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.265
But that's just
for the rendering.

00:11:45.265 --> 00:11:47.238
For all the motion control
 and all that,

00:11:47.238 --> 00:11:49.379
then I'd rather have...

00:11:49.379 --> 00:11:52.400
I'd rather have an Euler
or an axis angle rotation

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.085
or a quaternion, and not use

00:11:55.085 --> 00:11:57.480
a matrix for that.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.240
I just need the matrix at
the very end for the GPU side.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.513
And that's just trying
to process the final image.

00:12:03.513 --> 00:12:05.654
- Yeah, because you need
to combine the rotation

00:12:05.654 --> 00:12:08.165
with the translation.
- Translations and a scale.

00:12:08.165 --> 00:12:09.696
- So multiply them all out,
so they do

00:12:09.696 --> 00:12:11.365
have to be a matrix in the end.

00:12:11.365 --> 00:12:13.353
- Yeah.
 - The nice thing about

00:12:13.353 --> 00:12:16.469
quaternions is that
they smoothly interpolate

00:12:16.469 --> 00:12:19.620
between one orientation
and another.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.705
And I do think that
matrices kind of, like...

00:12:22.705 --> 00:12:24.500
- You can't do that.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.696
You can't do a spherical,
linear interpolation

00:12:27.696 --> 00:12:29.937
on a matrix,
but we can slurp

00:12:29.937 --> 00:12:31.847
quaternions.
- Slurp a quat.

00:12:31.847 --> 00:12:33.359
- Yeah, it's...
I don't know if we can

00:12:33.359 --> 00:12:35.614
slurp an Euler,

00:12:35.614 --> 00:12:37.855
but that does sound like
something that (bleep).

00:12:37.855 --> 00:12:39.455
(laughing)

00:12:39.455 --> 00:12:41.695
- Yeah, we need that drink,
Cassidy, in the coffee shop,

00:12:41.695 --> 00:12:43.369
the Euler special.

00:12:43.369 --> 00:12:45.400
And a gimbal locks
when you're trying to drink it.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.703
- Yeah.
- We know what happens when...

00:12:48.703 --> 00:12:51.520
- What if you wanted to rotate
the cup towards your face?

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.808
Wouldn't a...
Wouldn't a slurp help?

00:12:53.808 --> 00:12:55.344
(laughing)

00:12:55.344 --> 00:12:57.640
So, yeah, maybe we can
summarize, then.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.865
So you're spinning right now
on planet Earth.

00:13:00.865 --> 00:13:03.480
Planet Earth has an axis
 of rotation,

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.891
and it goes through
360 degrees every day.

00:13:06.891 --> 00:13:10.227
But there are certain axes
that are more kind of intuitive

00:13:10.227 --> 00:13:13.257
for us, the X, Y, Z axes.

00:13:13.257 --> 00:13:15.571
Or your brother would say...

00:13:15.571 --> 00:13:17.990
- Yaw, pitch, and roll.
- Yaw, pitch, and roll.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:19.910
But the problem is gimbal lock.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:22.240
So thank you so much
 for showing us

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.257
how animators are struggling
with gimbal lock

00:13:25.257 --> 00:13:27.495
using Euler angles.

00:13:27.495 --> 00:13:29.909
Now, you can get
out of gimbal lock

00:13:29.909 --> 00:13:32.041
by just having some more numbers

00:13:32.041 --> 00:13:33.575
to represent rotations.

00:13:33.575 --> 00:13:36.323
So instead of three,
if you have four numbers,

00:13:36.323 --> 00:13:38.518
like an axis angle,

00:13:38.518 --> 00:13:41.665
or a quaternion,
or even a rotation matrix,

00:13:41.665 --> 00:13:44.752
You can get out a gimbal lock.

00:13:44.752 --> 00:13:46.430
But I guess the issue
with that is that they're

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.491
not as intuitive.
- Not as intuitive.

00:13:48.491 --> 00:13:50.975
You can't deal with
the timing as well

00:13:50.975 --> 00:13:54.361
for an artist that's trying
to tell a story,

00:13:54.361 --> 00:13:56.255
but not trying to tell math.

00:13:56.255 --> 00:13:59.164
- I know.
It's hard to win an Oscar

00:13:59.164 --> 00:14:01.662
if you're using
straight quaternions

00:14:01.662 --> 00:14:04.730
to get the emotions.
And then, yeah.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.130
But in the end,
we have to turn to Scott.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:09.687
Everything has to be a matrix
 in order for

00:14:09.687 --> 00:14:11.530
us to get through this pipeline.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:13.744
- Or at least everything
has to be in

00:14:13.744 --> 00:14:16.196
a math form that I can
understand so I can code it.

00:14:16.196 --> 00:14:17.978
But thank you, Paul.
- You're welcome.

00:14:17.978 --> 00:14:20.000
- Thank you.
- It's always fun.